WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.325 --> 00:00:03.573
Imagine building one of the world's most loved toy brands…

2
00:00:04.025 --> 00:00:05.810
then almost losing everything.

3
00:00:08.314 --> 00:00:12.012
By 2004, LEGO was in its worst financial crisis.

4
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:13.688
Its business had become complicated,

5
00:00:13.808 --> 00:00:16.702
expensive, and increasingly difficult to manage.

6
00:00:16.873 --> 00:00:20.719
That year, it reported a net loss of nearly two billion Danish kroner.

7
00:00:21.231 --> 00:00:24.099
LEGO had expanded into too many directions.

8
00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:26.981
More products, more complexity, more costs.

9
00:00:27.161 --> 00:00:28.964
It was trying to become everything,

10
00:00:29.105 --> 00:00:31.545
while losing focus on what made it special.

11
00:00:32.107 --> 00:00:33.676
Then came a major change.

12
00:00:33.848 --> 00:00:37.178
In 2004, Jørgen Vig Knudstorp became CEO.

13
00:00:37.368 --> 00:00:40.505
His mission wasn't to make LEGO bigger in every direction.

14
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.601
It was to make the business stronger.

15
00:00:43.331 --> 00:00:45.829
LEGO started cutting unnecessary complexity,

16
00:00:46.143 --> 00:00:49.139
selling non-core assets, reducing costs,

17
00:00:49.269 --> 00:00:52.600
and returning its attention to its core product: the brick.

18
00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:55.968
It also worked to reconnect with the customers who loved it.

19
00:00:57.305 --> 00:00:58.583
The turnaround worked.

20
00:00:58.774 --> 00:01:03.117
By 2015, LEGO's revenue had reached 35.8 billion

21
00:01:03.237 --> 00:01:04.099
Danish kroner.

22
00:01:04.289 --> 00:01:06.450
A company that had once faced a serious fight for

23
00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.656
survival had rebuilt itself into one of the world's leading toy businesses.

24
00:01:11.325 --> 00:01:14.621
Here's the lesson: growth isn't always about doing more.

25
00:01:14.777 --> 00:01:17.362
Sometimes, it's about getting back to what you do best.
